✅ Sidebar and Layout Validation
Positive Test Cases
Verify all sidebar navigation links redirect to the correct pages.
Verify the sidebar layout adjusts correctly across screen sizes (desktop, tablet, mobile).
Verify clicking “Log out” displays a confirmation alert.
Verify the “Log out” option successfully logs the user out and redirects to the login screen.
Verify the user's name and profile picture are displayed as per requirements.
Verify the UI adjusts correctly for various resolutions (responsive design).
Verify all navigation and interactions are possible using only the keyboard.
Verify cross-browser compatibility: Chrome, Edge, Opera, Safari, Brave, and Internet Explorer.

❌ Negative Test Cases
Attempt to navigate using broken or unavailable sidebar links.
Test layout breakage on non-standard resolutions.
Try pressing “Log out” without internet connection — check error handling.
Verify profile image placeholder displays when the image fails to load.
Simulate unsupported screen reader behavior to detect accessibility issues.

✅ Event Page Validation
Positive Test Cases
Verify the initial event page state loads without any events or opportunities.
Verify correct messages are shown when no events or opportunities exist.
Verify single-click on “Create Event” button opens the event creation form.
Verify the filter functionality correctly narrows down the event/opportunity list.
Verify the notification bell shows unread notifications.
Verify the search bar filters events correctly by name or keyword.

❌ Negative Test Cases
Double-click “Create Event” and verify no duplicate action is triggered.
Click “Create Event” as a new (unauthorized) user and ensure proper warning is displayed.
Check the search bar with special characters or invalid inputs.
Verify filters with no matching results show appropriate "No Results" message.
Verify event/opportunity cards don't load when API fails — check fallback UI.

✅ Create Event Page Validation
Positive Test Cases
Verify a new event is saved when all required fields are valid.
Verify “Connect” section allows adding communication/contact methods.
Verify a valid event name (max 50 chars) and description (max 5000 chars) are accepted.
Verify alphanumeric and whitespace data is accepted in event name/description.
Verify unique event names are saved successfully.
Verify proper validation for online vs. in-person event type.
Verify category dropdown works and saves selected value.
Verify correctly placed and styled error messages on validation failures.

❌ Negative Test Cases
Click “Create” without filling any field — verify required field errors.
Double-click “Create” without input — ensure no duplicate submission.
Enter only special characters or only numeric data in name/description — verify rejection.
Enter a name identical to an existing event — check for duplication error.
Submit with either name or description empty — verify validation.
Submit corrupted data in form (e.g., script injections) — verify sanitization.
Check validation messages for all fields — spelling, grammar, and placement.

✅ Date & Time Field Validation
Positive Test Cases
Enter valid date format (MM/DD/YYYY) and verify acceptance.
Edit a previously selected date and save successfully.
Select valid future date to create an event.
Use calendar widget to navigate months and select a specific day.
Verify correct behavior during leap years (e.g., Feb 29).
Verify the widget supports time zone-aware selection.
Verify the calendar is responsive and works on all screen sizes.
Verify the calendar displays correct language (if multi-language supported).

❌ Negative Test Cases
Enter invalid date formats (e.g., 13/40/2025, 00/01/2025) — verify rejection.
Attempt to create an event with past date — validate restriction.
Simulate invalid year inputs or letters in date fields.
Test multiple calendars on the same page for conflicts.

✅ URL Field Validation
Positive Test Cases
Enter a valid HTTPS URL — verify it’s accepted.
Enter HTTP URL — verify acceptance but log a warning if applicable.
Encode special characters in URL and verify redirection works.
Handle URLs with anchors and query parameters properly.

❌ Negative Test Cases
Enter invalid URL formats (e.g., "example", "htp://site") — check rejection.
Enter unsupported protocols (e.g., FTP) — verify validation failure.
Enter malformed or incomplete URLs — verify proper error messages.

✅ Image Upload Validation
Positive Test Cases
Upload valid image files (JPG, PNG, JPEG) within size limits.
Ensure only one image is allowed — reject multiple file uploads.
Allow canceling an in-progress upload.
Display progress indicator during upload.
Ensure upload is accessible via keyboard navigation.
Validate retry functionality after a failed upload.
Show image preview after successful upload.

❌ Negative Test Cases
Upload unsupported file formats (e.g., .exe, .svg) — verify rejection.
Upload images exceeding the size limit — validate error message.
Simulate network loss during upload — validate retry or error message.
Upload corrupted or malicious files — verify rejection and security handling.
Attempt upload when “Upload” button is disabled — verify no action occurs.

✅ Price Textbox Validation
Positive Test Cases
Validate default values in “From” and “To” fields.
Enter a valid numeric range where “From” ≤ “To”.
Support edge cases like “From” = “To”.
Validate persistence of range on form submission.
Ensure keyboard accessibility and proper field labeling.
Confirm correct API call with range values on submission.

❌ Negative Test Cases
Enter non-numeric or special characters — verify rejection.
Leave either field empty and attempt submission — check validation.
Enter “From” > “To” — verify logic error message.
Enter out-of-bound values — verify maximum/minimum limits.
Check misaligned labels or validation messages against design specs.

🟡 Final Validation Checklist
All form validations must display clear, actionable error messages.
Accessibility compliance (WCAG) for screen readers, keyboard nav, color contrast.
Responsive behavior tested across major devices (iOS, Android, tablets, desktops).
API requests and responses validated using network tools.
All error states must be logged appropriately.
